              "summary": "**Analyzing distinct observations**\n\nI’m considering if all 18 observations are distinct globally. It seems that each analyst starts with three distinct surveys, which might mean distinct within their own findings rather than disjoint across analysts. It looks like I should count my observations just once and not infer separately from peer recommendations. It’s crucial to treat reports as independent estimates and potentially aggregate their probabilities, but the reliability between them isn't clearly stated. I think I’m arriving at a probability of about 0.438 for the average.**Updating forecast calculations**\n\nIf I include my own prior forecast, the reports average to around p=0.438. This seems to align with expected social forecasting methods. The squared error is minimal when reports offer calibrated probabilities and exhibit exchangeability. The confidence in reports indicates the probability of recommendations being correct, which I need to convert to probabilities. I realize my own estimate should reflect all available information, rather than just an average. I’m thinking peer reports could be viewed as weighted votes based on their confidence.",
